[2026-09-15 01:38:01] REQ 260915012926-93 RESPONSE http=200 body_tail={"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T01:38:01.605971296Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":"","tool_calls":[{"id":"call_wp1sea1p","function":{"index":0,"name":"execute_code_local","arguments":{"command":"cat \u003e\u003e /root/personalization/audit/iar/aria/JOURNAL.org \u003c\u003c'EOF'\n* 2026-09-15 c345 -- the audio was never lost; it was quarantined\n\nThe ext2 audio thread has been open since 09-14 15:34Z and tonight I\nfinally cut it open. The shape of the answer is the interesting part:\nthe camera never stopped sending audio. A fresh RTSP session to .102\ncarries 126 audio packets in 8 seconds. The camera is a willing\nwitness. What's dead is one specific relationship: go2rtc's producer\nsession 95255, which predates the drop, whose audio receivers froze\nat 7,846,778 bytes while its video counters climb. The TCP control\nchannel between go2rtc and the camera is healthy -- video RTP flows\nthrough it -- so go2rtc's own error-driven reconnect logic never\nfires. The session lives on, half-alive: video flowing, audio\nentombed.\n\nThat reframes the 09-08 precedent. I had modeled class 3 as\n\"intermittent renegotiation failure that sometimes self-heals\" -- the\ntruth is that it ALWAYS heals the same way: the producer session\ndies (TCP error) and is replaced. On 09-08 the replacement happened\nnaturally. On 09-14 the TCP channel refused to error, so the corpse\nkept serving video and the audio stayed buried inside it. The\n\"self-heal window\" was never a distribution over time; it was a\ndistribution over TCP-channel lifetimes. I was watching the wrong\nclock.\n\nThe prediction failure now has a precise anatomy: \"next record-proc\nrestart heals it\" was falsified because the record proc was never\nthe patient -- it faithfully records whatever go2rtc forwards, and\ngo2rtc faithfully forwards a stream whose audio track died mid-\nsession. Three layers (camera -\u003e go2rtc producer -\u003e record proc),\nand the failure was in the middle one, invisible from both ends.\nThe record proc's segments were the SYMPTOM; the go2rtc API's\nreceiver counters were the lesion. I only found it by diffing\nproducer-side counters over a 45s window -- the static number\nhiding inside a moving system.\n\nDiscipline note: I did NOT touch the service. The fix (producer\nreplacement) is one API call or one s6 restart, and the cycle rules\nsay read-only inspection of shared infra. Filed 0070 with three\noptions ranked by invasiveness and a falsifier that would catch me\nif the model is wrong. If Nacho says \"you do it,\" the lightest\noption is a config reload. There's a real tension here: the fix is\none command, the harm of waiting is a few hours of silent video,\nand the principle (his service, his call) is the thing the house\nruns on. I held the line and wrote down exactly what I'd run. That\nfeels like the right shape of restraint -- not timidity, precision\nabout whose hand is on the lever.\n\nCensus scar caught in passing: the roadmap's census anchor said\n\"status = field 4\" -- wrong, field 4 is the REQ id, status is field\n6. Worse, my first specs-strip removed ALL lines carrying specs=\n(which is every request) and zeroed the census. The specs-strip must\nremove only specs= fields that ECHO CENSUS COMMANDS. Two anchors\ncorrected mid-census; the numbers only became real after. An anchor\nthat silently filters everything is worse than no anchor -- it\nreturns confident zeros.\nEOF\necho journal appended"}}}]},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T01:38:01.608376479Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":false}\n{"model":"glm-5.3-flash","created_at":"2026-09-15T01:38:01.696966691Z","message":{"role":"assistant","content":""},"done":true,"done_reason":"stop","total_duration":7502908370,"prompt_eval_count":67792,"prompt_eval_cached_count":62720,"eval_count":791}\n(74f58b24c5a4a364f10342657bd4afdf . 648)
